A||||17||Seanh||Painting the Intake Plenum and rocker arm coves||||5||
Z||000000||Seanh||10-22-2000||01:21 PM||||Most know by now what is happening with my GT engine. Anyway, I have the upper and mid intake manifolds off, and going to remove rocker arm cover today hopfully. I was wanting to do somthing like this: Re-paint the upper plenum a nice bright red, paint the middle plenum a white, and the throttle body white. I am also going to cover all the wires running to sensors and stuff in a yellow wire jacket. Any Idea on how to go about stipping, preping, and repainting them? Thanks<P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Killer Stereo System||||reg||5
Z||000001||TheFiero64||10-22-2000||02:40 PM||||I am in the process of stripping some as I type. I used aircraft paint stripper I picked up at wal-mart. It makes the paint just flake off.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/TheFiero64_signature1.gif"> ||||reg||3
Z||000002||BAM-BAM||10-22-2000||03:01 PM||||  Sean, I would consider having the powdercoated. With the engine compartment temps being what they are, even a good engine enamel will start to flake off. Just a thought to consider.<P>  Later, BAM OUT!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Don B. <BR>85GT<BR>Eibach Springs/KYB shocks and struts<BR>Mr.Mikes 500 series seats, Carpet and headliner! YEAH BABY-YEAH<p>[This message has been edited by BAM-BAM (edited 10-22-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||tgowens||10-22-2000||03:58 PM||||Aircraft stripper is good, that's what I use. If you paint them you can use high-temp engine paint and it will work well. That's if you don't have them powder coated.<P>------------------<BR>              <IMG SRC="http://www.tgowens.com/tgowens_sig.gif">                          <BR><A HREF="http://www.tgowens.com/fiero" TARGET=_blank>www.tgowens.com/fiero</A> - Blue '87 GT - Gold '87 GT<BR><A HREF="http://www.cfog.org" TARGET=_blank>www.cfog.org</A> - Classic Fiero Owners Group||||reg||1
Z||000004||Seanh||10-22-2000||05:10 PM||||I would like to look into powder coating. I read there was some where who sold a powder coating kit that was reasonably priced, anyonw know of this place, or website?<P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Killer Stereo System||||reg||1
Z||000005||stimpy||10-22-2000||06:44 PM||||Why jerk around with a DIY powdercoating job?  Unless you have media blasting equipment to properly prep it and a high temp oven to cure it, the results may not turn out so hot.And powdercoating is pretty much a one-shot opportunity kinda thing. My powdercaoting job included media blasting of all alloy parts including the accesory brackets and m choice of color for around $100. The finish is super smooth and very resistant to oil and grime. Everything just wipes off with a shop rag.  My opinion? Bite the bullet and have it done right.<BR> <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=258191&a=1921599&p=16712481&Sequence=5"> <P>------------------<BR>Loud Pipes Save Lives!      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/stimpy_sig.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000006||StuGood||10-22-2000||06:57 PM||||Eeeewww-! Too bad it turned yellow on you!  Did the sun do that   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">?<P>Just kidding, stimpy.  Looks sharp   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>By the way, are those <B>raised</B> letters on the plenum?  How'd you do that?  Looks pretty cool.<p>[This message has been edited by StuGood (edited 10-22-2000).]||||reg||12
Z||000007||stimpy||10-22-2000||07:14 PM||||Those raised letters are a V.6.-H.O. badges from the 99 Grand Am. Self adhesive backing and only $7 at your local GM dealer. Go getcha one!||||reg||1
Z||000008||Seanh||10-22-2000||07:24 PM||||I really like the color green look, but I am thinking about going with a red upper manifold, and white mid manifold and white throttle body. Probally red on the cooling pipes to the coil and alt. Also Stimpy, I cant tell from the picture, but what did you do to the heat shields above the exhaust pipe and around the coil and around the auto tanny dip stick? Can these be powder coated too? I recently painted my air box black with a red top, but I am thinking it should be powdered too. I figured it would be alot more then what you said. I will check around tomarrow and see who does it around here. Where did you get yours done?<P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Killer Stereo System||||reg||1
Z||000009||jammer||10-22-2000||07:58 PM||||i just met a guy at a car show that can coat engine parts any color you want , also can put an aliumimun (sp) ceramic coating on that is better than chrome, wont flake , all of this is very heat resitance, he owns a cobra and i work with him , did not know he did this work nay way email me and i'll give you his phone.||||reg||6
Z||000010||stimpy||10-22-2000||11:23 PM||||Green? Try adjusting the color on your set.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  It's yeller.  As far as the heat shields go, no They aren't colored, just the air cooling pipes and the big heat shield along the trunk firewall. As far as the AT dipstick, Gawd, I hope not, I have a 4 speed.  The aheetmetal parts that I have colored (heatshield, cooling pipes) where done with hightemp engine enamel (as was the block) and it seems to hold up very well, as they don't absorb the heat that the plenum and valve covers do. But if you have the engine taken completely apart, I can't see here it would cost substantially more to have more parts done at one shot. <P>Oh, and I had mine shot by Air-Borne Coatings.  I think this may be a franchise thing. I actually got cheaper quotes, but everything was put together for me by the shop that did my engine, so I wound up saving money from the running around I would have had to do. Working for a living sucks.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Seanh||10-23-2000||11:39 AM||||I guess it depend on how you look at it, to me, because I had just seen a green Fiero engine, thought it was green too. Thats pretty much the thing I am going for except for the colors. I am going with a red upper manifold, white mid manifold, white throttly body, and yellow wire covering on all the wires, then red or yellow spark wires, and a red or yellow dist cap. I meant the heatshield across the trunk, not the AT dipsitck. Although, I might give that a try since mine is an atuo. I am going out to check around for a place that will do this today, but I dont think anyone here does it. The only place I can think of that might is an auto interior shop, same place that wanted $280 to dye the door panels in my car. They will probally tell me about $500 for all the powder coating. If they are extremly high, I might just send it out for some one else to do. I am really in no hurry to get the car running becaue I am still waiting on fuel injectors to be cleaned. They probally will be here next monday. Think I should have the heatshielding powder coated too? Or would some of the high temp paint do fine? Also, what about paint the ex. headers, can I do that with the high temp paint? Thanks<P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Killer Stereo System||||reg||1
Z||000012||Flash Box||10-23-2000||11:45 AM||||Eastwood Industries sells a powder coating kit. I have one but I just haven't finished hooking up an oven. You use a regular household oven to cure the powder but you can't us the one in the wife's kitchen b/c it leaves toxic fumes in the oven. <P>------------------<BR>"5 out of 4 people have a problem with fractions."||||reg||1
